# ~/.skhdrc — skhd v0.3.9 + yabai v7.1.25
# Reload with: skhd --restart-service
#
# Full paths are used deliberately: skhd runs under launchd, which does not
# inherit your shell PATH, so a bare `yabai` may not resolve.
#
# LAYOUT OF THIS FILE
#   ctrl + <key>   -> spaces (always live, no mode)
#   tap caps_lock  -> enters "window mode", where bare letters act on windows
#   ctrl + cmd     -> send the focused window to a space AND follow it
#   ctrl + alt     -> send the focused window to a space and STAY
#   nothing on plain alt, because alt is reserved for app toggling
#
# ctrl+alt is shared with Karabiner's buttonless-drag chord, deliberately —
# see the note above those bindings for what that costs.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  MODES
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Syntax: :: <name> [@] [: <command run on entering>]
# The `@` makes the mode CAPTURE keypresses — anything not bound below is
# swallowed rather than passed to the app. That is what makes bare `h` safe
# to bind, and also why the on-screen indicator matters: without it you can
# be sitting in a mode that eats your typing.
#
# The indicator is a cheat sheet drawn centred on screen by Hammerspoon (hs.ipc
# is loaded in init.lua; yabaiHudShow/Hide are defined in mouse-drag.lua, which
# init.lua requires). It also parks the mouse out of the way while shown, since
# the mode is modal. If Hammerspoon isn't running these fail silently and the
# mode still works — you just lose the cheat sheet.
#
# THE CHEAT SHEET IS GENERATED FROM THIS FILE — nothing to keep in sync.
# init.lua re-parses it on every show, so edits appear immediately. Two
# markers drive it, both ordinary skhd comments so they change no behaviour:
#
#   #== <title>   starts a section on the sheet
#   #= <label>    the description shown for that binding
#
# A binding with no `#=` is simply left off the sheet. Rows whose command ends
# in `skhd -k escape` are detected automatically and marked as leaving the
# mode — that isn't annotated by hand either.
:: default : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudHide()' > /dev/null 2>&1
:: window @ : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow()' > /dev/null 2>&1
# Reached from window mode by pressing `m`. Same capture-all behaviour; the HUD
# switches to its move variant (title + follow/stay hint + space picker).
:: move @ : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
# Reached from window mode by pressing `x`. Unlike move (which reuses the
# window panel with a chip), the HUD REPLACES its sections with the ones
# parsed from the `power <` bindings at the bottom of this file.
:: power @ : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("power")' > /dev/null 2>&1
# Reached from window mode by pressing `a`. Like power, the HUD swaps in its
# own panel, parsed from the `arrange <` bindings below the move mode.
:: arrange @ : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("arrange")' > /dev/null 2>&1

# ── enter / leave ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PRIMARY: a quick TAP on caps_lock. Karabiner's `to_if_alone` on that key
# emits f19 when caps_lock is pressed and released within 250ms with nothing
# in between; holding it is still plain Hyper, untouched and with no added
# latency (the `to` fires on key-down, `to_if_alone` only on key-up).
#
# f19 by keycode because skhd has no function-key literals — kVK_F19 = 0x50.
# No Mac keyboard has an F19 key, so nothing else can ever send this.
#
# `fn -` IS REQUIRED and is the whole reason a bare `0x50` silently does
# nothing. macOS tags every function-key event with kCGEventFlagMaskSecondaryFn,
# so the tap arrives as `code=0x50 flags=[fn]`, and skhd matches modifiers
# EXACTLY — a binding with no modifier can never match an event that has one.
# Verified with a Hammerspoon event log; Karabiner was doing its job all along.
#
# The other two are insurance for flag combinations we have not observed.
fn - 0x50    ; window
0x50         ; window
hyper - 0x50 ; window

# Summon / dismiss the scratchpad from ANYWHERE, no mode needed: hold caps
# (hyper) and hit p. Same letter as the `p` row inside window mode; claiming
# and releasing (shift-p) still live there. Free of conflicts: no Keyboard
# Maestro macro or Karabiner rule uses full hyper + a letter (checked
# 2026-08-16). When no window has been claimed the wrapper beeps + toasts
# instead of failing silently into skhd's log.
hyper - p : ~/.config/yabai/summon-scratchpad.sh pad

# Four ways out, because being stuck in a capture mode is the failure case.
# Tapping caps_lock again toggles back out — same gesture in and out.
window < fn - 0x50    ; default
window < 0x50         ; default
window < hyper - 0x50 ; default
window < escape       ; default
window < q            ; default
window < return       ; default

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  WINDOW MODE
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Rule of thumb: navigation and layout tweaks KEEP you in the mode so you can
# chain them; property toggles EXIT afterwards via `skhd -k escape`, which
# trips the `window < escape` binding above.

#== navigate
# ── directional focus (stays in mode) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Move focus by DIRECTION rather than by app — the main payoff of tiling.
# DIR_SEL := north | east | south | west (doc/yabai.asciidoc:100).
# h/j/k/l sit in the same physical spots on AZERTY as on QWERTY.
window < h : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus west     #= Focus
window < j : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus south    #= Focus
window < k : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus north    #= Focus
window < l : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --focus east     #= Focus

# ── move windows around the tree (stays in mode) ──────────────────────────
# --swap exchanges two windows in place. (--warp is the other option: it
# re-parents the window, reshaping the tree rather than trading positions.)
window < shift - h : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap west   #= Swap
window < shift - j : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap south  #= Swap
window < shift - k : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap north  #= Swap
window < shift - l : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --swap east   #= Swap

# ── rotate the layout (stays in mode) ─────────────────────────────────────
# COUNTER-INTUITIVE: 270 is clockwise, 90 is counter-clockwise. The docs don't
# say which is which; this was derived from window_node_rotate() in src/view.c
# and confirmed by testing (a window on the right moves to the bottom under
# --rotate 270, which is a clockwise quarter turn). Don't "fix" these numbers.
#
# Only does anything on a bsp space — a float space has no tree to rotate.
window < r         : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --rotate 270   #= Rotate right
window < shift - r : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --rotate 90    #= Rotate left

# Reset all split ratios to equal, after dragging borders around.
# The shipped example (examples/skhdrc:89) uses `0`, but a literal 0 is
# unreachable on AZERTY without Shift, so this uses the physical key position.
window < 0x1D : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --balance   #= Balance splits

#== window
# ── fullscreen zoom (stays in mode, chip while active) ────────────────────
# Fills the space with the focused window WITHOUT leaving your layout or
# triggering a macOS fullscreen animation. Press again to drop back in place.
# Used to exit the mode, which meant its HUD chip was never visible at the
# moment you flipped it; now it behaves like the other stateful toggles.
window < f : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudFlip("fullscreen")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle zoom-fullscreen   #= Fullscreen

# ── stateful toggles (flip and STAY, so the HUD chip updates in place) ─────
# These are on/off states you may want to flip and see reflected, so instead of
# leaving the mode they re-render the HUD (which re-queries state and inverts the
# row). Escape/caps still leaves when you're done.
#
# Sticky: the window follows you onto every space. Native yabai toggle.
# Flip the HUD chip FIRST (instant, from cache), then run the real toggle -- so
# the highlight tracks the keypress instead of waiting on yabai.
window < s : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudFlip("sticky")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle sticky   #= Show on all Spaces

# Always on top: no native toggle in v7 (`topmost` was replaced by the
# `--sub-layer` setter), so this script reads the state and flips it.
# Beeps if there's no focused window. Chip flipped first, same as sticky.
window < t : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudFlip("topmost")' > /dev/null 2>&1; ~/.config/yabai/toggle-topmost.sh   #= Always on top

# Drop shadow: native yabai toggle (scripting addition, like opacity below).
# The chip inverts while the shadow is OFF -- the engaged, non-default state.
window < d : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudFlip("shadow")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle shadow   #= Drop shadow

# Opacity: 100% <-> 75%. yabai only has a setter, so the script reads the
# current value and flips; restoring clears the override (--opacity 0.0)
# instead of pinning 1.0. Chip inverts while translucent.
window < o : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudFlip("opacity")' > /dev/null 2>&1; ~/.config/yabai/toggle-opacity.sh   #= Opacity 75%

# `x` swaps the HUD for the power panel: system reloads, cleanup sweeps, and
# global (all-window) switches. Same capture behaviour; x toggles back.
window < x ; power

# PIP: scales the window to fill the display, honouring your padding
# (window_manager.c:2410 -> scripting_addition_scale_window). Note it SCALES
# rather than resizes — the window's contents are magnified, they do not
# re-layout, which is what makes it different from zoom-fullscreen on `f`.
# Requires the scripting addition, so it silently does nothing if the SA has
# been lost after a Dock restart.
#
# On `i` rather than the `k` you asked for, because k is focus-north and part
# of the hjkl core. Move it if you prefer — it is this one line.
window < i : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle pip; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= PiP (scale to display)

# App Expose for the focused window's app: pick a sibling window visually.
# One-shot like pip/fullscreen -- Expose is its own modal overlay, so the HUD
# leaves rather than sitting on top of it.
window < e : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle expose; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= App Exposé

# ── nudge / size the focused window ───────────────────────────────────────
# The ARROW keys and SPACE are handled in Hammerspoon (mouse-drag.lua), not here.
# Arrows move the window through the Accessibility API for a smooth glide with no
# drop-shadow flicker (yabai's animated --move flickers the shadow); holding a key
# slides it, Shift dashes. Space cycles the window through three centred sizes --
# small / medium / large, scaled to the display -- and then back to the size it
# started at. The HS event tap swallows both while the HUD is up.

#== scratchpad
# ── scratchpad (exit after) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# A scratchpad is a labelled window you summon and dismiss from ANY space.
# Assigning one automatically makes it floating and unmanaged.
#
# Usage: focus the window you want (a terminal is the classic choice), press
# shift - p once to claim the "pad" label, then p from anywhere to show/hide
# it. Press shift - p again on that window to release the label. Outside the
# mode, hyper - p (bound globally near the top of this file) does the same
# show/hide. The HUD shows which app currently holds the pad on the `p` row,
# and dims the show/hide + recover rows while nothing is claimed.
window < p         : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --toggle pad; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Show / hide
window < shift - p : ~/.config/yabai/toggle-scratchpad.sh pad; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Claim / release

# If a scratchpad ever gets lost off-screen, this forcefully brings every
# scratchpad window back to the front. Grouped with the other `p` bindings;
# ctrl is free to use here because the mode captures everything anyway.
window < ctrl - p : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m window --scratchpad recover; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Recover lost scratchpads

#== spaces
# ── switch to a space by number, then leave the mode ──────────────────────
# Top-row AND numpad keys 1-9, bound by KEYCODE so they work on AZERTY without
# Shift (same reason 0x1D is used for `balance` above). No `#=` label on
# purpose: the HUD renders these as the space indicator row at the bottom
# (current bright, the rest dimmed), not as list rows. focus-space.sh beeps if
# the target space doesn't exist, matching the global ctrl bindings.
#
# Two labelled rows here: `m` starts the move-to-space chord, `a` the arrange
# chord (both defined further down).
window < m ; move   #= Move to Space: digit or ←→ (⇧ = stay)
window < a ; arrange   #= Arrange windows: T C S N (⇧ = this app only)
# tab / shift-tab cycle spaces without leaving the mode. HANDLED IN HAMMERSPOON
# (mouse-drag.lua), not here: skhd fired a yabai call plus a full `hs -c` CLI
# invocation on every key repeat, which choked when tab was held down. The HUD's
# event tap swallows tab first, so this line never actually runs -- it exists so
# the binding still shows up on the cheat sheet (which is generated from this
# file). Keep the label in sync with the Hammerspoon behaviour.
window < tab : true   #= Next Space (⇧ = previous)

# Toggle tiling for the CURRENT space only (bsp <-> float). This is a
# per-SPACE property -- every window on the space tiles or floats together --
# which is why it lives under this heading and not under `window`. Other
# spaces keep whatever they were; resets to the ~/.yabairc default when yabai
# restarts. The HUD underlines the numbers of bsp spaces in its top row and
# dims the tiling-only rows (swap/rotate/balance/fullscreen) while floating.
# The script sends the mode-leaving escape ITSELF, but only when restoring to
# float -- entering bsp keeps window mode (and the HUD) up so you can
# immediately tune the fresh layout. The trailing `hs` call re-renders the
# HUD from live yabai state (underlines + dimming); it no-ops when the script
# already dismissed the HUD.
window < b : ~/.config/yabai/toggle-layout.sh; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudRefresh()' > /dev/null 2>&1   #= Tile / float this Space

# Padding & gaps for the CURRENT space, flipped together (they're one visual
# idea: breathing room around and between tiles). Stays in the mode so you can
# flip straight back. Only meaningful on a bsp space, so the HUD dims it in
# float. No chip: the space query doesn't report padding/gap state.
window < g : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --toggle padding; /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --toggle gap   #= Padding & gaps
# top row -> kVK_ANSI_1..9 (5 and 6 are swapped; 7/8/9 jump around)
window < 0x12 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x13 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 2; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x14 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 3; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x15 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 4; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x17 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 5; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x16 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 6; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x1A : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 7; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x1C : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 8; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x19 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 9; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
# numpad -> kVK_ANSI_Keypad1..9 (0x5A skipped: that is F20, not a keypad key)
window < 0x53 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x54 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 2; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x55 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 3; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x56 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 4; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x57 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 5; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x58 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 6; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x59 : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 7; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x5B : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 8; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"
window < 0x5C : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 9; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  MOVE MODE — reached with `m` from window mode
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Pick a destination space for the FOCUSED window:
#   digit         -> move & follow (land on that space with the window)
#   shift + digit -> move & stay   (window goes, you don't)
#   left / right  -> move & follow to the ADJACENT space (wraps around)
#   shift + arrow -> throw it there & stay put
# Both top row and numpad, by keycode (AZERTY-safe). move-window-to-space.sh
# beeps AND toasts if the target space doesn't exist or the window can't be moved.

# ── ways out (cancel without moving) ──────────────────────────────────────
# `m` toggles move OFF: back to window mode (HUD stays, the m chip clears).
# escape / q / return / caps tap leave the HUD entirely.
move < m            ; window
move < escape       ; default
move < q            ; default
move < return       ; default
move < 0x50         ; default
move < fn - 0x50    ; default
move < hyper - 0x50 ; default

# ── adjacent space: arrows ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# left / right walk the window to the adjacent space. NOT BOUND HERE, for the
# same reason as tab: these are meant to be HELD (keep tapping until you land on
# the space you want), and a skhd binding would spawn the move script plus an
# `hs -c` HUD refresh on every auto-repeat. Handled in Hammerspoon instead --
# see hudThrowWindow in mouse-drag.lua, which throttles and keeps the HUD up.

# ── move & FOLLOW: bare digit ─────────────────────────────────────────────
move < 0x12 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x13 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x14 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x15 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x17 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x16 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x1A : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x1C : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x19 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x53 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x54 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x55 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x56 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x57 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x58 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x59 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x5B : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < 0x5C : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9 follow; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1

# ── move & STAY: shift + digit ────────────────────────────────────────────
move < shift - 0x12 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x13 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x14 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x15 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x17 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x16 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x1A : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x1C : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x19 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x53 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x54 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x55 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x56 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x57 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x58 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x59 : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x5B : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1
move < shift - 0x5C : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudShow("move")' > /dev/null 2>&1

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  ARRANGE MODE — reached with `a` from window mode
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Re-arrange every visible window on the CURRENT space (all apps), or with
# shift only the frontmost app's windows. Implemented in Hammerspoon
# (window-management.lua, arrangeWindows): windows come from yabai's list, so
# hidden helper panels never count and nothing is ever minimized. One-shot:
# each action leaves the mode so you can see the result.
#   t  tile     one row up to four windows, a grid beyond that
#   c  cascade  equal-sized windows stepping down and right
#   n  neat     large, lightly varied windows fanned along a diagonal --
#               overlapping, but every title bar stays in view
#   s  scatter  random positions, sizes kept
# Only works on a FLOATING space; on a bsp space it toasts and does nothing.

#== this space
arrange < t         : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 3 -c 'arrangeWindows("tile")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Tile
arrange < c         : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 3 -c 'arrangeWindows("cascade")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Cascade
arrange < n         : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 3 -c 'arrangeWindows("neat")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Neat (tidy overlap)
arrange < s         : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 3 -c 'arrangeWindows("scatter")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Scatter (random)

#== this app only
arrange < shift - t : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 3 -c 'arrangeWindows("tile", "app")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Tile
arrange < shift - c : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 3 -c 'arrangeWindows("cascade", "app")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Cascade
arrange < shift - n : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 3 -c 'arrangeWindows("neat", "app")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Neat
arrange < shift - s : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 3 -c 'arrangeWindows("scatter", "app")' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"   #= Scatter

# ── ways out ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `a` toggles back to the window panel; everything else leaves entirely.
arrange < a            ; window
arrange < escape       ; default
arrange < q            ; default
arrange < return       ; default
arrange < 0x50         ; default
arrange < fn - 0x50    ; default
arrange < hyper - 0x50 ; default

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  SPACES — always live, outside any mode
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# ctrl is safe to claim globally: these replace the macOS "Move left/right a
# space" shortcuts you disabled, and ctrl+digit isn't used by apps.

# ── switch spaces: ctrl + arrows ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Switching is instant (no Mission Control slide) because it goes through the
# scripting addition rather than the public API.
#
# The `||` fallback provides wraparound: `--focus next` errors with
# "could not locate the next space" on the last space, so we jump to the first.
# `2>/dev/null` only hides the harmless "could not locate the next space"
# message that yabai prints before the fallback runs — the wrap still happens.
ctrl - left   : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus prev 2>/dev/null || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus last
ctrl - right  : /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus next 2>/dev/null || /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai -m space --focus first

# ── jump straight to a space: ctrl + top row ──────────────────────────────
# Bound by HARDWARE KEYCODE, not by character. b∫On AZERTY the top row produces
# & é " ' ( § è ! ç à and the digits need Shift, so a literal `ctrl - 1` would
# be unreachable. Keycodes refer to the physical key position instead, so these
# work regardless of layout (and keep working if you switch to QWERTY).
#
# Keycodes are from kVK_ANSI_* in Carbon's Events.h. Note they are NOT
# sequential — 5 and 6 are swapped, and 7/8/9/0 jump around. Don't "fix" them.
#
# These go through focus-space.sh, which beeps (system alert sound) when the
# target space doesn't exist instead of failing silently.
ctrl - 0x12   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 1   # & (kVK_ANSI_1)
ctrl - 0x13   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 2   # é (kVK_ANSI_2)
ctrl - 0x14   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 3   # " (kVK_ANSI_3)
ctrl - 0x15   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 4   # ' (kVK_ANSI_4)
ctrl - 0x17   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 5   # ( (kVK_ANSI_5)
ctrl - 0x16   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 6   # § (kVK_ANSI_6)
ctrl - 0x1A   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 7   # è (kVK_ANSI_7)
ctrl - 0x1C   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 8   # ! (kVK_ANSI_8)
ctrl - 0x19   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 9   # ç (kVK_ANSI_9)

# à = an alternative to ctrl - tab: back to the space you came from. HANDLED IN
# HAMMERSPOON (space-toggle.lua), not here, for two things skhd cannot do: it
# fires again on every key repeat (holding the key ping-ponged between the two
# spaces), and it has no key-UP event, so "hold to peek, snap back on release"
# is impossible. Hammerspoon's tap owns 0x1D and 0x52 with a BARE ctrl only --
# the ctrl+cmd / ctrl+alt window-movers below still belong to skhd.

# ── the same, on the NUMPAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# kVK_ANSI_Keypad0..9. Note 0x5A is skipped — that is F20, not a keypad key.
#
# UNCERTAIN UNTIL TESTED: macOS tags keypad events with
# kCGEventFlagMaskNumericPad, and skhd matches modifiers EXACTLY — the same
# trap that made a bare `0x50` silently fail for f19. skhd has no `numpad`
# modifier to declare, so this works only if skhd masks that bit away rather
# than comparing it. Arrow keys carry an intrinsic flag too and `ctrl - left`
# works fine, which is the reason to expect masking. If these do nothing, the
# fallback is a Karabiner rule remapping the keypad to keys skhd can match.
ctrl - 0x53   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 1   # keypad 1
ctrl - 0x54   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 2   # keypad 2
ctrl - 0x55   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 3   # keypad 3
ctrl - 0x56   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 4   # keypad 4
ctrl - 0x57   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 5   # keypad 5
ctrl - 0x58   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 6   # keypad 6
ctrl - 0x59   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 7   # keypad 7
ctrl - 0x5B   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 8   # keypad 8
ctrl - 0x5C   : ~/.config/yabai/focus-space.sh 9   # keypad 9
# keypad 0 -> toggle. Also Hammerspoon's, see the note on 0x1D above.

# ── back to the previous space (toggle) ───────────────────────────────────
# ctrl - tab is HANDLED IN HAMMERSPOON (space-toggle.lua) together with ctrl - à
# and ctrl - keypad 0; see the note on 0x1D above for why.

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  TAKE THIS WINDOW WITH ME — ctrl + cmd
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Same key layout as the ctrl bindings above, plus cmd: wherever ctrl would
# send YOU, ctrl+cmd sends you AND the focused window.
#
# ctrl+cmd is genuinely free — a scan of com.apple.symbolichotkeys found zero
# enabled macOS shortcuts using that combination. Worth re-checking after a
# major OS update, since macOS shortcuts win over skhd (which is why the
# Mission Control ctrl+arrow defaults had to be turned off by hand).
#
# The script resolves next/prev to an absolute index BEFORE moving. Chaining
# `window --space next && space --focus next` would land you one space too
# far, because "next" gets re-evaluated after the window has already moved.
ctrl + cmd - left  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh prev follow
ctrl + cmd - right : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh next follow

ctrl + cmd - 0x12  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1 follow   # &
ctrl + cmd - 0x13  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2 follow   # é
ctrl + cmd - 0x14  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3 follow   # "
ctrl + cmd - 0x15  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4 follow   # '
ctrl + cmd - 0x17  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5 follow   # (
ctrl + cmd - 0x16  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6 follow   # §
ctrl + cmd - 0x1A  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7 follow   # è
ctrl + cmd - 0x1C  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8 follow   # !
ctrl + cmd - 0x19  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9 follow   # ç

# à = the space you came from, mirroring `ctrl - à`
ctrl + cmd - 0x1D  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh recent follow

# ── the same, on the NUMPAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────
ctrl + cmd - 0x53  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1 follow
ctrl + cmd - 0x54  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2 follow
ctrl + cmd - 0x55  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3 follow
ctrl + cmd - 0x56  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4 follow
ctrl + cmd - 0x57  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5 follow
ctrl + cmd - 0x58  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6 follow
ctrl + cmd - 0x59  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7 follow
ctrl + cmd - 0x5B  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8 follow
ctrl + cmd - 0x5C  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9 follow
ctrl + cmd - 0x52  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh recent follow

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  SEND THE WINDOW AWAY, STAY HERE — ctrl + alt
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Same layout again, without `follow`. Free of macOS: the only arrow-key
# system shortcuts are Mission Control's ctrl+fn+left/right, and you disabled
# those.
#
# KNOWN SIDE EFFECT, and it is the reason ctrl+alt was avoided everywhere
# else. Karabiner turns left-ctrl + left-option pressed within 60ms of each
# other into button1 + ctrl/alt, which is yabai's buttonless drag. So:
#
#   * These bindings MATCH either way. Karabiner keeps ctrl+alt asserted on
#     everything it emits, so the arrow still arrives as ctrl+alt+arrow
#     whether or not the chord fired.
#   * But when the chord DOES fire, a mouse button is held down for as long
#     as you hold the keys. `mouse_replay_click off` means no stray click is
#     ever delivered, so with hands on the keyboard nothing happens — but
#     move the mouse mid-hold and you will drag the window under the pointer.
#
# Two ways to avoid it if it bites: press ctrl and option more than 60ms
# apart (the chord needs them simultaneous), or raise
# basic.simultaneous_threshold_milliseconds' opposite — lower it further.
ctrl + alt - left  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh prev
ctrl + alt - right : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh next

ctrl + alt - 0x12  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1   # &
ctrl + alt - 0x13  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2   # é
ctrl + alt - 0x14  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3   # "
ctrl + alt - 0x15  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4   # '
ctrl + alt - 0x17  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5   # (
ctrl + alt - 0x16  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6   # §
ctrl + alt - 0x1A  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7   # è
ctrl + alt - 0x1C  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8   # !
ctrl + alt - 0x19  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9   # ç
ctrl + alt - 0x1D  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh recent

# ── the same, on the NUMPAD ───────────────────────────────────────────────
ctrl + alt - 0x53  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 1
ctrl + alt - 0x54  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 2
ctrl + alt - 0x55  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 3
ctrl + alt - 0x56  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 4
ctrl + alt - 0x57  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 5
ctrl + alt - 0x58  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 6
ctrl + alt - 0x59  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 7
ctrl + alt - 0x5B  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 8
ctrl + alt - 0x5C  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh 9
ctrl + alt - 0x52  : ~/.config/yabai/move-window-to-space.sh recent

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PRAATPAAL
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# F18 (kVK_F18 = 0x4F) focuses the Warp tab of the Claude session that last
# spoke a praatpaal cue. Both variants because of the same fn-flag trap
# documented at the f19 binding above.
fn - 0x4F : ~/repos/praatpaal/praatpaal goto
0x4F      : ~/repos/praatpaal/praatpaal goto

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  POWER MODE (window mode -> x)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Rarely-needed, bigger-hammer commands. The HUD replaces the window panel
# with the sections below (same #== / #= markers, parsed per mode).

#== system
# Restart yabai. Leaves the mode FIRST: the restart tears down yabai's query
# API, so the HUD would otherwise sit there showing stale state.
power < y : /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"; /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai --restart-service   #= Restart yabai

# Restart skhd itself. The HUD is hidden first because skhd forgets its mode
# state on restart -- without the hide, the panel would linger with no mode
# behind it. The forked shell survives the restart long enough to finish.
power < s : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudHide()' > /dev/null 2>&1; /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd --restart-service   #= Restart skhd

# Reload the Hammerspoon config. Also leaves the mode first: the reload kills
# the Lua side of the HUD mid-flight.
power < h : /opt/homebrew/bin/skhd -k "escape"; sleep 0.2; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 2 -c 'hs.reload()' > /dev/null 2>&1   #= Reload Hammerspoon

# Reload the scripting addition (passwordless via /etc/sudoers.d/yabai; see
# ~/.yabairc). The cure for "yabai stopped moving windows after Dock died".
power < a : sudo /opt/homebrew/bin/yabai --load-sa; /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 2 -c 'require("toast").show([[Scripting addition reloaded]], { duration = 2 })' > /dev/null 2>&1   #= Reload the scripting addition

#== cleanup
# Quit every GUI app that has no windows anywhere. Checked against yabai's
# window list, NOT Accessibility -- an AX-dark app still has real windows and
# must not be killed. Finder and Hammerspoon are always spared. Defined in
# mouse-drag.lua; toasts what it quit.
power < w : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 3 -c 'powerQuitWindowlessApps()' > /dev/null 2>&1   #= Quit apps without windows

# Undo every per-window override (opacity / shadow / sub-layer) in one sweep.
# Sticky and the scratchpad label are left alone -- see the script header.
power < r : ~/.config/yabai/reset-overrides.sh   #= Reset all window overrides

#== global
# All-window shadow flip (yabai config) -- the big brother of window mode's
# per-window `d`. Chip while shadows are globally OFF.
power < d : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudFlip("gshadow")' > /dev/null 2>&1; ~/.config/yabai/toggle-global-shadow.sh   #= Hide all shadows

# yabai window animations: 0.15s <-> instant. Instant is the screen-recording
# setting; a yabai restart reapplies ~/.yabairc's 0.15. Chip while instant.
power < n : /opt/homebrew/bin/hs -A -t 1 -c 'yabaiHudFlip("ganim")' > /dev/null 2>&1; ~/.config/yabai/toggle-animations.sh   #= Disable window animations

# ── ways out ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `x` toggles back to the window panel; everything else leaves entirely.
power < x ; window
power < escape       ; default
power < q            ; default
power < return       ; default
power < 0x50         ; default
power < fn - 0x50    ; default
power < hyper - 0x50 ; default
